/**
 * Epic 2 Wave 1 — Application dictionary seed: API resources and processes
 * (CORE-ROADMAP.md §4.2 "Pendaftaran halaman dan aksi yang sudah ada").
 *
 * The roadmap states: "13 modules, 162 permissions, and about 42 RBAC consumer
 * files are registered AUTOMATICALLY as the opening wave." This migration is
 * that automation for the two objects that describe the server surface:
 *
 *   - app_processes       — one row per permission (164 as of this migration).
 *                           A "process" in the dictionary is an action a
 *                           person can be granted: submit, approve, verify,
 *                           export. In Abixell One every such action is gated
 *                           by exactly one permission code, so the permission
 *                           set IS the action set.
 *   - app_api_resources   — one row per HTTP route the server actually serves
 *                           (617 as of this migration), enumerated from
 *                           Fastify own onRoute hook rather than by
 *                           scanning source, because a large share of the
 *                           surface is registered inside loops and cannot be
 *                           reconstructed by reading the route files.
 *
 * Both objects carry module_code, and nothing in the schema derives it. The
 * rule set that does lives in code, not here:
 *   apps/api/src/domain/configuration/module-attribution.ts
 * The catalogue below is generated from that file; the parity test in
 * apps/api/tests/dictionary-seed-parity.test.ts regenerates both lists from
 * the live registry and the live permission table and fails if any row here
 * still disagrees. Treat this file as a snapshot of a derivation, never as the
 * place a mapping is decided.
 *
 * Access definitions are code-owned ("Batas wewenang" — super admin may not
 * edit access definitions from the UI), so no tenant-facing write path touches
 * these rows. Tenants receive a copy through the same trigger-plus-backfill
 * pattern the route windows use.
 *
 * No permission is introduced (164 before and after), no role_permissions row
 * is written, and no DDL beyond the two indexes and the two trigger objects.
 */

